WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.357 --> 00:00:07.030
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: Welcome.

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.102
Thanks for joining us today.

00:00:09.102 --> 00:00:11.310
We're going to be talking
about analyzing distributed

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.790
traces to find performance
bottlenecks in your system.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:18.066
I'm Bryan Zimmerman, product
manager with Google Cloud.

00:00:18.066 --> 00:00:19.510
FRANCIS BOGSANYI:
And I'm Francis.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:21.610
I work on distributed
tracing in Shopify,

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:26.643
and I'm also a maintainer
of OpenTelemetry Ruby.

00:00:26.643 --> 00:00:28.810
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: In order
to speak about performance

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.880
troubleshooting,
first, let's talk

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.460
about performance problems.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.600
These are problems with the
latency or compute utilization

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.160
of your services,
and these are things

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.170
that will impact your user's
experience of your product

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.710
and your bottom line.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.420
These are often
difficult because there

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:50.050
is no clear error or event
that has caused the issue.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.840
Instead, they may be
an optimization that

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.822
has existed in your
system for a while,

00:00:55.822 --> 00:00:57.780
or there could be a
problem that has creeped up

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.962
or is otherwise buried.

00:00:59.962 --> 00:01:02.420
FRANCIS BOGSANYI: Performance
problems in large distributed

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.060
systems can be
surprising, and causality

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:09.232
is often missing or buried
in other telemetry signals.

00:01:09.232 --> 00:01:11.690
A couple of real examples we
found with distributed tracing

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:15.740
in Shopify, an RPC was
executed twice unintentionally

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:18.660
due to a quirk in the
network architecture.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.750
And volume in memcache that grew
in size without anyone noticing

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.180
and then suddenly became
popular during a flash sale,

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.730
exhausting network
link capacity.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:30.723
Both of these jumped up really
quickly from trace data.

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:32.140
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN:
Today we're going

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.570
to be highlighting a couple
key tools from the cloud

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.360
observability toolset.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.530
First is Cloud Trace.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:41.180
This is a distributed
tracing tool

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.790
that is particularly useful
for latency scenarios,

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.950
but it's also useful
in any scenario

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:51.310
where a downstream service is
affecting upstream behavior.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.240
Then there's Cloud Profiler.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.677
This is a continuous
profiling application

00:01:55.677 --> 00:01:57.760
that helps you find exactly
what part of your code

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:58.980
is responsible for an issue.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:06.190
These exist as part
of a larger toolset.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.860
This toolset is based off the
same planet scale platform

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.500
that powers Google's internal
observability, so you

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.630
know it's been battle tested.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:16.780
In addition, it comes with
zero day configuration,

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.910
out of the box for
key Google services.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.010
So for those who
aren't familiar,

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.640
let's talk about what
distributed tracing is.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.175
Here's an example
application architecture.

00:02:27.175 --> 00:02:29.050
Your users may communicate
with the front end

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.083
through load balancers.

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:33.250
That will communicate in
turn to multiple back ends,

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.040
including databases.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.410
However, today,
your application is

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:42.800
more likely to look like this,
many small micro services

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.110
working together
for a common goal.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.080
This is a great
application architecture

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.870
that becomes very
difficult to troubleshoot.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.850
Distributed tracing
allows you to understand

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:54.470
exactly how the
request has moved

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.410
through this environment,
which definitely simplifies

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:58.532
troubleshooting scenarios.

00:02:58.532 --> 00:02:59.990
FRANCIS BOGSANYI:
At Shopify, we're

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:03.020
often known for our devotion
to our core monolith.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.270
In reality though, there is
an increasingly large set

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.520
of services orbiting
that monolith,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.190
and performance is impacted
by all services involved

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:14.760
in processing their request,
including third-party services.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.620
Distributed tracing
provides visibility

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.840
into the performance of
these increasingly deep,

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.930
distributed systems that is
almost impossible to achieve

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:24.075
any other way.

00:03:24.075 --> 00:03:29.440
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: Next, let's
review, continuous profiling.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.050
Continuous profiling
helps specifically

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:35.570
with what happens in
a particular service.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:38.780
The intent is to identify the
method or section of code that

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:43.010
is contributing to the issue or
the source of the optimization.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:46.400
Cloud Profiler takes
profiles of your application

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:49.650
at every function
call in a sampled way.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:53.690
This means it's perfectly
safe to run in production.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.660
These profiles are laid
out on a flame graph where

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:59.630
it's visually extremely
easy to understand

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:01.832
how these functions
play together

00:04:01.832 --> 00:04:03.290
and where the cause
of an issue is.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.950
When we talk to customers,
the troubleshooting process

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.590
typically follows these steps.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.410
You start with an issue and
refine that issue as much

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:19.810
as possible using
the metrics you have,

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:23.050
then you try to identify the
service that's responsible.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:26.710
This is particularly important
for distributed systems.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:29.410
Further refining to the
method or part of your code

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.780
where the issue
exists is extremely

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:35.160
important for efficient
troubleshooting.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.270
And then finally, looking at
deep information such as logs

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:40.580
and debugging tools,
help you come up

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.320
with a viable
hypothesis for what

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.910
might be causing the issue.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.220
This is, of course, required
before any resolution can

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.460
be attempted, which needs
to be pushed and confirmed

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.387
using the same metrics that
identified the problem for you.

00:04:53.387 --> 00:04:55.470
FRANCIS BOGSANYI: In
practice, we can treat traces

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:57.810
as contextualized logs.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.640
Typically, trace
instrumentation initially

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.130
focuses on the edges
in a service graph

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.920
where communication
happens between services.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.840
Performance problems
show up as large spans

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:09.130
with large gaps
between child spans,

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.610
but we don't know
what's going on.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.630
So the hypothesis
part of our process

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:17.060
involves incrementally
filling in those empty spaces

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.130
by adding instrumentation
to our code

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:21.380
until we gain some
insight into what

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.100
is different about this path.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.600
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN:
Now, let's see this

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.240
in action using Google
Cloud's observability suite

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.420
of products.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.540
We're going to show
you a series of demos

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:36.120
on our demo application
online boutique.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.120
We'll take you from problem
to service to method

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.780
to hypothesis starting with
identifying and refining

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:45.758
the problem.

00:05:45.758 --> 00:05:47.300
FRANCIS BOGSANYI:
We're going to show

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:50.270
how to use Cloud Monitoring
to find and refine

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:53.750
the problem you're
attempting to investigate.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:55.280
As you can see here,
we already have

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:56.750
an incident related to latency.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:04.250
You can see from looking
at the policy details

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:06.170
that we've actually
defined this alert

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:11.270
to be 90th percentile latency
sustained for one minute.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:13.880
This kind of alert
configuration ensures

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:17.690
that only real problems
generate alerts and wake up

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:18.530
our on-call tech.

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:23.020
To understand the
problem further,

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:25.520
we're going to open up one of
our pre-configured dashboards,

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.637
in this case, the
Performance dashboard.

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:35.270
We can see a definite
spike in latency

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:37.900
in our server latency heat map.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:41.380
This can be shown both from the
aggregate percentile lines as

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:43.227
well as the distribution itself.

00:06:43.227 --> 00:06:44.810
Let's drill into the
inflection point.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.430
At this point, we definitely
understand what to investigate,

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:57.840
which is GRPC server latency.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.580
Next, we're to use
distributed tracing

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:04.070
to understand the source
of that latency spike.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:06.550
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: With the
problem well understood,

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:09.010
it's time to identify
the service where

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:12.178
the issue most likely exists.

00:07:12.178 --> 00:07:15.173
FRANCIS BOGSANYI: To investigate
the cause of this latency,

00:07:15.173 --> 00:07:17.465
we're going to use a new
feature called Example Traces.

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:23.800
This allows us to
overlay sampled traces

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.320
onto the latency heat map
from within the monitoring

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.480
dashboard.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:31.930
Here we can drill into
the specific requests

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:35.800
that generated those metric
data points of interest.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:38.080
In this instance,
it's quite obvious

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:39.640
to see that most of
the time is spent

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.760
during the getProduct operation.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:46.210
We can check other requests to
see if it's a similar pattern,

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:47.110
and indeed, it is.

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:53.930
To validate our
hypothesis, let's check

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:57.980
some 50th percentile traces
from before the spike.

00:08:11.643 --> 00:08:13.060
To investigate
this further, we're

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:14.143
going to open Cloud Trace.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:21.390
Here we can further
dig into the traces

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:25.200
that exemplify this problem.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.870
This allows us to filter for
additional details, including

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:33.070
label values and see
the trend over time.

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:36.179
As you can see, the
visual representation

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:39.090
of your entire request
that trace provides

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:41.970
allows you to easily see
where the problem is.

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.020
In this case, as
mentioned earlier,

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:47.760
it seems to be in the product
catalog service getProduct

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.280
operation, which
is done repeatedly

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.380
and has been taking
a much longer time

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.242
than looking at traces
from before the spike.

00:08:55.242 --> 00:08:56.700
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN:
Kellyanne is going

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:58.710
to show you how you
can use Google Cloud

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:02.160
Profiler to identify
where in the code

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.162
this issue or
optimization can be found.

00:09:05.162 --> 00:09:07.300
KELLYANNE: Now, we will
use Google Cloud Profiler

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:10.300
to investigate which
functions in your service code

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:12.100
could be slowing it down.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:14.170
The default view
of profiler shows

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:18.190
you a plane graph representation
of your service profile.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.590
In this plane graph, we
can get a bird's eye view

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:23.530
of where the service is
spending most of its time.

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:26.690
Here we have selected the
product catalog service,

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:29.950
and we are specifically
looking at the CPU

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:32.530
time for this service.

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.600
Just by looking at
the plane graph,

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:37.000
we can understand
that most of the time

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:41.710
is spent in this stack,
which is the GRPC

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:43.660
receiver for this service.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:45.790
Walking down this
stack, we can see

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:51.490
that POS catalog is where you
are spending most of your time.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:57.400
Opening the top function's table
and sorting by that total time,

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:02.320
we can confirm that
68% of the total time

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:04.270
is spent in POS catalog.

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:06.310
Walking down the
stack some more,

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:11.140
we can see that this time
is spent in actually JSON

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:13.160
Unmarshaling function.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:17.050
So this should be the first
focus of our optimization

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.795
efforts.

00:10:19.795 --> 00:10:22.823
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: To form
a viable hypothesis,

00:10:22.823 --> 00:10:24.490
we're going to use
logging and debugging

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:26.980
tools included with
Google Cloud observability

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:28.215
suite of products.

00:10:28.215 --> 00:10:30.070
FRANCIS BOGSANYI: We're going
to jump to the related logs

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:31.153
for this particular trace.

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:38.470
In the previous demo, we found
that the cause of the problem

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:42.100
was most likely within the
product catalog service.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:44.170
To dig into this
further, we're going

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:49.270
to filter to that particular
Kubernetes resource pod.

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:52.210
This can be done easily
at the click of a button.

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.820
And immediately,
via the histogram,

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:57.010
it's obvious that
something has changed

00:10:57.010 --> 00:10:59.750
around the time of this issue.

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:01.450
Let's jump to the
start of this problem

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:03.390
to see what's happening.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:07.370
And similar to what we
saw within Profiler,

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:11.080
this service is successfully
parsing the product catalog

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:13.900
very, very often and
repeatedly, certainly

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:16.840
more than it was previous
to the issue starting.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.180
This confirms our
hypothesis that the problem

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:23.290
is related to parsing
of the product catalog.

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:25.810
To further dig into this,
we can inspect the messages

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:29.540
from our logs, correlate that
with the behavior of our code

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:31.330
via debugger and our IDE.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:38.080
Cloud Debugger
allows you to inspect

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:40.510
what's happening within
your running application

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:43.690
by injecting snapshots
or log points

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:46.540
whenever you like in your code.

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:51.270
We'll show this in action by
going to our currency service,

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:57.940
and let's take a
snapshot at line 155.

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:00.280
What we can see here on
the right-hand side is,

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:04.960
instantly, we're able to see
details of the local variables

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:08.000
at the time the
snapshot was taken.

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:10.150
Now, what if you only
want to take that snapshot

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:11.680
in certain circumstances?

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:15.070
Maybe I'm chasing an issue
with only a certain region

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:17.260
or a certain user.

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:18.825
Well, you can add conditions.

00:12:18.825 --> 00:12:20.200
These are written
in the language

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:21.970
of the code that
you're debugging

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:24.970
or expressions, which
will print certain aspects

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:29.350
of your application
as you write them.

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:31.880
Now, maybe I don't want a
snapshot that occurs once,

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:35.160
but I want to on a recurring
basis take a log point.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:36.610
Well, you can do that.

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:38.170
You can inject log
points that will

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:41.410
be run every time the code
is hit, not just once.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:44.470
And again, these can be written
with any condition, written

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:47.020
in the language of the
code you're debugging,

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:50.020
and with any message.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:52.660
The editor allows you
to do this easily.

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.360
You can write whatever log point
you want as long as it will not

00:12:55.360 --> 00:13:00.640
affect the writing byte code and
it meets certain memory and CPU

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:03.970
footprint requirements.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:05.830
These features allow
you to understand

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:09.093
what's happening within your
actual production application.

00:13:09.093 --> 00:13:10.510
This can be a game
changer when it

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:13.870
comes to understanding
and debugging issues

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:16.210
that only happen on production.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:21.160
It also makes it much easier for
operators, SREs, or developers

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:23.860
to troubleshoot issues
without having to wait

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:25.060
for long deployment cycles.

00:13:29.497 --> 00:13:32.240
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: The
demos you've just seen

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:33.920
gives you a highly
guided experience

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:35.660
to troubleshoot
performance problems

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:37.400
using Google Cloud's
observability suite

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:41.230
of products, but sometimes
the answer is not that simple.

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:43.510
There isn't a single
request or even

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:47.260
a set of logs that shows you
exactly where the problem is.

00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:50.680
This is where understanding
the system and its requests

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:52.957
in aggregate is
extremely important.

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:58.220
In order to help
with this, we've

00:13:58.220 --> 00:14:00.500
recently released a
feature where traces

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:03.380
can be exported to BigQuery.

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:06.140
Any label that you send
into your span data

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:07.820
gets written as
unique columns, which

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:09.590
makes searching even easier.

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:11.660
This allows you to use
the power of BigQuery

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:13.690
to perform custom analysis.

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:15.290
It also allows
you to retain data

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:18.213
for much longer than
the included 30 days.

00:14:18.213 --> 00:14:19.630
Now, I'm going to
hand things over

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:21.550
to Francis, who's
going to explain

00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:25.140
how this kind of aggregate
analysis is done at Shopify.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.360
FRANCIS BOGSANYI: At
Shopify, we use the query

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:29.040
for aggregate trace analysis.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:32.220
I'm going to gratefully
demonstrate how we do that.

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:35.010
Firstly though, why do we
need aggregate analysis?

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:36.810
The typical approach
to trace analysis

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:38.400
is to search for
interesting traces,

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:40.410
perhaps by service
and time frame

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:42.840
and either incrementally
refine our search until we

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.650
have a smallish set
of traces to look at

00:14:45.650 --> 00:14:48.500
or hope that the 1,000 or so
traces return to representative

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:52.170
of what we're looking for
and start clicking randomly.

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:54.192
By viewing a few
random traces, we're

00:14:54.192 --> 00:14:55.650
hoping that anything
interesting we

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:59.160
find is representative of
the larger set of traces.

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:01.080
Using a tool like the
query, however, we

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:02.940
can analyze all the
traces in the set

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:05.553
and allow it to surface
interesting information.

00:15:05.553 --> 00:15:07.470
We still often want to
look at complete traces

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:10.170
to provide more context,
but we'll have confidence

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:13.440
that the traces we look at
will be truly representative.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:15.450
If we want to use BigQuery
for trace analysis,

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:17.708
we have to get the trace
data into it somehow.

00:15:17.708 --> 00:15:19.500
There's a couple of
ways you could do this.

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:22.800
For example, at Shopify, we have
a slightly complex ingestion

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:26.130
pipeline using the OpenTelemetry
Collector, pops-up GCS,

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:28.680
and batch upload to be queried.

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:30.930
Google Cloud Trace
provides a simpler option

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:32.933
with Trace Exports.

00:15:32.933 --> 00:15:34.350
The scheme in the
Cloud Trace used

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:37.920
for trace data in the query is
determined by the trace V2 API

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:39.390
definition of span.

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:41.940
So it stores one span per
row with nested records

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:43.680
for span attributes.

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.690
Shopify schema is
similar but based

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:49.577
on the OpenTelemetry protocol
definition of span instead.

00:15:49.577 --> 00:15:51.660
If we're performing in-depth
analysis of something

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:53.730
relatively constrained,
our first step

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:55.230
is always to extract
the data we're

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:58.000
interested in into
its own table.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.150
This can drastically reduce
query costs, leverage

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:03.805
the query cache, and generally,
increase responsiveness.

00:16:03.805 --> 00:16:05.430
In this example,
we're going to analyze

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:08.690
synthetic load generated
against the test shop.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:11.510
Almost all of the units of work
performed by our core monolith,

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:13.220
whether request or
background jobs,

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:15.000
are scoped to a single shop.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:16.880
So we always tag
requests in jobs spans

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:19.330
with the [INAUDIBLE] shop ID.

00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:21.870
We know the time interval
for the load test and the ID

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:22.980
that the test shop.

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:25.270
Our table is partitioned
by start time,

00:16:25.270 --> 00:16:28.000
so we specify that
as our first photo.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:29.500
We're doing a bunch
of analysis that

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:32.087
has no obvious partitioning
or clustering characteristics,

00:16:32.087 --> 00:16:34.420
so we don't bother specifying
clustering or partitioning

00:16:34.420 --> 00:16:36.850
for our destination table.

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:39.040
We use the subquery to
identify trace IDs that

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:40.870
meet our requirements
and then copy

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:44.810
all those spans from those
traces to the new table.

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:47.850
Here we've extracted about
8 gigabytes and 13 and 1/2

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:51.130
million spans from a
22 terabyte partition.

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:54.900
This significantly reduces
the working set for analysis.

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:57.030
Let's start our analysis
with some basic queries

00:16:57.030 --> 00:17:00.337
to figure out the services
involved in read metrics.

00:17:00.337 --> 00:17:01.920
It doesn't get much
simpler than this.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:04.650
This gives us a list of
the services involved.

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:07.089
Read metrics are a
little more complicated.

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:10.490
Let's focus on one service,
the storefront renderer.

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:13.050
We'll count requests per minute,
filtering to the service,

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:15.359
and looking only
at surface spans.

00:17:15.359 --> 00:17:18.010
The key piece is here at
truncating the stop time

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:21.420
and grouping and ordering
by the truncated stop time.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:24.329
We can see around 45,000
RPM over the first half

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:27.030
of the test, followed by a
dip, and then an increase

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:30.977
to around 175,000 RPM
in the second half.

00:17:30.977 --> 00:17:33.060
I should note here that
we're done sampling traces

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:35.730
in the collectors, so we
saw a significant multiple

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:37.590
of this request
rate, but this serves

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:41.170
to show the general
shape of the load.

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:43.120
Note that we can trivially
adjust this query

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:46.380
to return a second request rate.

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:48.880
The big query console is
great for trying our queries,

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:50.950
but once we start building
queries like this,

00:17:50.950 --> 00:17:53.325
we want to easily
visualize the results.

00:17:53.325 --> 00:17:54.700
There are many
tools to integrate

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:57.460
with a query including Google
Sheets and Data Studio.

00:17:57.460 --> 00:18:01.840
At Shopify, we use Mode
Analytics for data analysis.

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:04.840
In Mode, we build our
SQL queries separately,

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:07.890
and then we can access the data
sets by name in a notebook.

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:10.950
Plotting our query
results is pretty trivial.

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:12.920
Here are the plots from
our earliest queries

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:14.820
and from a similar
query for request rate

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:16.920
to our core [INAUDIBLE] list.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:18.840
To calculate duration
as a distribution,

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:21.540
we bucketize by minute and
compute each percentile

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:23.040
over each minute.

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:24.960
We represent duration
in microseconds,

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:27.510
so here I'm converting
to milliseconds.

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:30.060
And here's what that
looks like as a plot.

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:33.160
Before we drill in further,
one more high-level perspective

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:34.680
we might like is
the relationship

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:37.070
between the services involved
and the relative volume

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:38.400
of traffic.

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:42.350
This is variously called a
service graph, or service map.

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:44.330
Here's what that looks
like in this example.

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:47.080
The key pieces we need to know
are the source destination

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:51.270
and request count for
each edge in the graph.

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:54.500
The query required for this
is a little more complicated,

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:56.437
but there's really two
things going on here.

00:18:56.437 --> 00:18:58.020
The first part of
this query considers

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:00.810
requests that are
represented by a single span.

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:02.723
These are typically
database or cache queries

00:19:02.723 --> 00:19:04.890
where we only have the
instrumentation on the client

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:06.377
side.

00:19:06.377 --> 00:19:08.460
The other set of requests
we're interested in have

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:11.820
two sides, so we grab the
set of [INAUDIBLE] spans

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:14.550
with the tracing span IDs
and the set of service spans

00:19:14.550 --> 00:19:16.470
with the trace and
parent span IDs

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.570
and then join them
on the trace ID

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:24.590
where the parent span ID at the
server is the client span ID.

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:28.350
Grouping gives us the request
count along each edge.

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:30.350
So let's go deeper and
break down request counts

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:32.400
and durations by component.

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:35.770
For request count, we query the
Rows Control, attribute as well

00:19:35.770 --> 00:19:38.650
and group by both start
time and the controller.

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:40.490
In some cases, the
attribute is missing,

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:42.730
so we replace it with unknown.

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:44.350
Plotting this gives
us a request rate

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:46.790
for the different controllers.

00:19:46.790 --> 00:19:48.760
Breaking down the
duration by controller

00:19:48.760 --> 00:19:50.590
is slightly more gnarly.

00:19:50.590 --> 00:19:52.340
I built this query in
the BigQuery console

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:54.440
by trial and error, and
a data analyst somewhere

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:55.815
is probably having
a fit watching

00:19:55.815 --> 00:19:57.830
me try to play one on TV.

00:19:57.830 --> 00:20:00.560
Regardless, some query extracts
a start time, controller,

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:02.670
and duration from
the relevant spans,

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:05.150
and then we can keep the
P95 partition by the start

00:20:05.150 --> 00:20:07.720
time and controller.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:10.700
The result is a little obscured
by an outlier controller, which

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:13.010
looks like the
null count control.

00:20:13.010 --> 00:20:15.690
We could ignore this
controller in the SQL query,

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:18.050
but it's easier to do
it here with pandas.

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:20.450
This produces a much
clearer breakdown.

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:22.970
Drill downs are also
relatively easy with pandas.

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:25.670
Here we drill down into the
collection's controller.

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:28.533
We can then easily contrast the
duration for this controller

00:20:28.533 --> 00:20:30.200
with the request rate
for the controller

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:32.248
from our earlier query.

00:20:32.248 --> 00:20:33.790
Now, we might want
to ask where we're

00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:37.530
spending time in aggregate in
request to this controller.

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:40.470
Let's start by querying all
the operations performed

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:42.340
during those requests.

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:45.420
We have 26 operations,
which is quite manageable.

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:47.990
We have the expected reticent
MySQL queries and template

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:50.210
parsing and rendering.

00:20:50.210 --> 00:20:52.515
Computing the workload
contribution of each operation

00:20:52.515 --> 00:20:54.140
is as simple as
something with duration

00:20:54.140 --> 00:20:56.390
and grouping by the span name.

00:20:56.390 --> 00:20:58.100
Ordering by the
cumulative duration

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:01.100
allows us to quickly
identify the heavy hitters.

00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:03.530
The top two are interesting
for the rest of these spans,

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:07.040
and we can clearly see MySQL
dominates followed by Redis.

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:09.080
We can further summarize
by database type

00:21:09.080 --> 00:21:13.010
to see the cumulative
MySQL in Redis times.

00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:14.530
In this service,
we also tag spans

00:21:14.530 --> 00:21:17.030
with the time spent in garbage
collection, which we can then

00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:18.780
extract and summarize.

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:21.110
We're interested to know
whether GC dominates any

00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:22.730
of the expensive operations.

00:21:22.730 --> 00:21:23.800
In fact, it doesn't.

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:26.840
It accounts for less than 1%
of time spent in MySQL spans

00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:30.650
and a little over 4% of
overall request time.

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:32.620
Alternatively, we can
look at the contribution

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:34.350
of different SQL queries.

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:36.610
In this service, we
tag MySQL query spans

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:38.770
with a digest of the
query template, which

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:40.852
makes it easy for us
to aggregate spans,

00:21:40.852 --> 00:21:42.310
and then we can
search for examples

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:45.070
and find the actual
queries in another tag.

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:47.890
Here we see one query
clearly dominates.

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:50.050
Finally, in the same way
we did with controllers,

00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:54.190
we can analyze request rate
and duration by arbitrary tags.

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:56.950
This is a request count by zone.

00:21:56.950 --> 00:21:59.530
And this is a P95
duration by zone.

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:00.980
We can see some
small differences,

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:02.890
but nothing especially exciting.

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:05.710
If we instead look at cache
hit versus misperformance,

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:07.780
we can see a more
distinct difference.

00:22:07.780 --> 00:22:09.400
And we can see
that our cache hit

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:13.265
rate is reasonable at over 96%.

00:22:13.265 --> 00:22:14.890
I hope that's given
you a taste of some

00:22:14.890 --> 00:22:17.200
of the types of analyses
that you can perform over

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:19.620
trace data using BigQuery.

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:21.340
The key elements are
getting trace data

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:23.820
into BigQuery, which you can
accomplish with Cloud Trace

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:27.070
Export, then extracting
interesting traces

00:22:27.070 --> 00:22:30.610
to a separate table, typically
based on a time window.

00:22:30.610 --> 00:22:32.590
This helps to
control query costs

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:35.080
and greatly increases
responsiveness.

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:37.030
And finally, it's
extremely helpful

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:39.260
to have a tool to view
the query results.

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:41.740
At Shopify, we use
Mode Analytics,

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:44.900
but there are many other tools
that integrate with BigQuery.

00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:47.170
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: Thank
you, Francis, and thank you

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:48.790
all for joining.

00:22:48.790 --> 00:22:51.890
Please feel free to reach out
for follow up, and as always,

00:22:51.890 --> 00:22:54.660
visit as a cloud.google.com.

